  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the stated hypothesis direction. For dependency_composite, I accept a dataset-specific association only when the signed effect is above the 0.15-unit threshold with uncertainty supporting a threshold-exceeding effect in the claimed direction. H37, MSI-high association, is now mature: discovery R73 gave signed +0.775 with interval +0.664 to +0.885, and independent validation R78 gave signed +0.866 with interval +0.693 to +1.040. Because H37 direction is -1, this corresponds to a raw lower composite dependency score in MSI-high samples, i.e. stronger measured dependency. Both discovery and validated intervals are far above the 0.15-unit threshold, so I accept H37 as a strong dataset-specific association. I keep H37 active because registered robustness refinements H106-H109 should assess whether the MSI-high association is explained by mutation burden, TP53 status, or screen quality. H2, STK11 loss in the broad eligible population, received scheduled validation R79: signed +0.062 with interval -0.052 to +0.177. The point estimate is far below threshold and the interval crosses zero, so the claimed negative-direction STK11 association is not supported. This confirms the earlier discovery null and H2 is rejected and closed. The response-deadline claim H40, PTEN loss among TP53-intact samples, gives a different pattern. Discovery R33 was threshold-positive, signed +0.259 with interval +0.169 to +0.349, but independent validation R53 is signed +0.246 with interval +0.113 to +0.379. The point estimate remains above threshold, but the validated interval includes values below 0.15 and near the threshold boundary. Given prior PTEN validation failure and the multiplicity of PTEN contexts, I do not accept H40. I leave it unresolved but close this TP53-intact PTEN branch because further stratum mining would add multiplicity without addressing the uncertainty. Overall supported dataset-specific associations remain SMARCA4 loss toward stronger dependency, TP53 loss toward weaker dependency, KEAP1 loss toward weaker dependency, and now MSI-high toward stronger dependency. STK11 adds to the negative/near-null set. PTEN remains a suggestive but unresolved predictor because several contexts show moderate positive point estimates without mature interval support above threshold. The next most informative analyses are the pre-registered MSI-high robustness comparisons H106-H109, not additional PTEN stratum mining. All conclusions are associations within the supplied dataset; they do not establish causality, single-gene knockout mechanism, or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Updated mature accepted dataset-specific associations: H3/H14 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency; H13 TP53 loss -> weaker dependency; H38 KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency; H37 MSI-high -> stronger dependency, now validated and accepted. H37 remains active for robustness tests H106-H109: low vs high mutation burden, TP53-intact, and high screen quality. These test whether the strong MSI-high effect is confounded by mutation burden, co-alteration patterns, or technical screen features. STK11 H2 is now rejected and closed after validation R79; broad STK11 does not support threshold-level dependency difference. PTEN remains unresolved and not accepted. H40 TP53-intact PTEN had positive discovery but validation lower bound below threshold, so closed unresolved. H94 final PTEN context also remained immature. Do not pursue further PTEN stratum mining unless an independent validation already exists. CDKN2A remains borderline/unresolved across several contexts, not accepted. Broad negative/near-null findings include STK11, KRAS, MYC amp, ARID1A, MTAP, RB1, EGFR amp, ERBB2 amp, BRAF V600E, PIK3CA, APC, SMAD4, NF1, BRCA2, research markers, sex positive claim, media serum 10 vs 5, Avana main effect, matched omics/CRISPR QC main effect, and tested interactions for the large predictors. Next analyze queued MSI-high refinements H106-H109; avoid new PTEN claims. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable claims."
